{:receipt :kotobase/where-the-hydrate-cpu-goes
 :date "2026-08-17"
 :graph "kotobase-protocols-v2"
 :head "bafyreiehotlnqouj5u35gqh535ctxr52smxiphdjhiydtmivc2v6tqx3ye"
 :decision "ADR-2608172100"

 :question
 "Which materialised view should kotobase build first? The hypothesis under test:
  the biggest materialisable thing is not a query view but the LocalStore SEED
  itself -- a pure function of the chain head CID, content-addressed and
  immutable -- so materialising it collapses the hydrate to a range read and
  needs no knowledge of the query mix."

 :verdict
 {:hypothesis :right-about-the-key-wrong-about-the-size
  :because "The hydrate is not dominated by fetching 84 blocks. It is dominated by
            turning stored EDN text back into Clojure values, which a materialised
            seed still has to do -- the consumer is a SYNCHRONOUS
            kotobase.local/LocalStore over a plain Clojure map. Block fetch +
            decode + tree walk + novelty merge is 23% of the CPU; the value decode
            is 64%."
  :and "The single largest lever is not a view at all: 91 documents carrying inline
        object bodies are 85% of everything every request parses, and since
        2026-08-10 no s3 request on this graph can even reach them."}

 ;; ------------------------------------------------- 1. cost, in workerd, measured
 :workerd-cpu
 {:how "`wrangler tail kotobase-protocols-worker --format json`, reading the
        cpuTime/wallTime fields of each event. atproto is a public-read surface
        (core/public-read-surfaces), so a hydrating request needs no credential.
        Two windows, 14 hydrating requests."
  :hydrating-request {:cpu-ms [2329 4416] :wall-ms [2851 6184] :n 14 :outcome "ok"}
  :diag-hydrate-route {:cpu-ms [1 3] :wall-ms [1 3]
                       :reading "the diagnostic really is O(1) and cannot cause the
                                 work it reports"}
  :reading "CPU is 68-85% of wall. This path is CPU-bound, not I/O-bound -- which
            matters because the isolate block cache already removes most of the
            I/O and a seed materialisation is aimed at the same half."}

 ;; ------------------------------- 2. the diagnostic that shipped this morning lied
 :io-wait-ms-is-not-a-cost
 {:finding "`/_diag/hydrate`'s `ms` under-reported by up to 112x."
  :mechanism "Date.now() inside a Cloudflare Worker advances only at I/O
              boundaries -- the clock is frozen across synchronous execution, a
              deliberate timing-attack mitigation. A delta taken around the hydrate
              therefore measures accumulated I/O WAIT and charges nothing for CPU."
  :method "Eight paired probes: one hydrating request immediately followed, ON THE
           SAME CONNECTION, by a read of /_diag/hydrate, with `wrangler tail`
           running. Each sample's `at` falls inside its request's window, which is
           what makes the pairing an observation rather than an assumption."
  :rows
  [{:probe "pair-1" :cpu-ms 2802 :wall-ms 4119 :reported-ms 820}
   {:probe "pair-2" :cpu-ms 2488 :wall-ms 3790 :reported-ms 819}
   {:probe "pair-3" :cpu-ms 4163 :wall-ms 5883 :reported-ms 1251}
   {:probe "pair-4" :cpu-ms 4416 :wall-ms 6184 :reported-ms 1822}
   {:probe "pair-5" :cpu-ms 3890 :wall-ms 4745 :reported-ms 43}
   {:probe "pair-6" :cpu-ms 4046 :wall-ms 5092 :reported-ms 36}
   {:probe "pair-7" :cpu-ms 2682 :wall-ms 3809 :reported-ms 780}
   {:probe "pair-8" :cpu-ms 2511 :wall-ms 3701 :reported-ms 898}]
  :the-inversion "The two worst under-reports are the ones where the isolate block
                  cache was WARM -- there was no I/O left to time. The number is
                  smallest exactly when it is most misleading."
  :fixed "Renamed io_wait_ms, `cost_note` served in the body, blocks_from_cache /
          blocks_from_r2 added. Deployed; both directions observed live:
          io_wait_ms=1220 from_cache=0 from_r2=84, then the same work at
          io_wait_ms=44 from_cache=84 from_r2=0."}

 ;; --------------------------------------------- 3. the split, over production bytes
 :phase-split
 {:fidelity
  {:bytes "REAL. All 118 blocks reachable from the production head were fetched
           read-only (scripts/fetch-graph-blocks.cljs) and seeded into an in-memory
           bucket under their real CIDs; the head pointer is production's own chain
           CID. Nothing was re-committed."
   :self-check "The engine verifies block CIDs on decode. The first version of the
                harness seeded Node Buffers, whose `.buffer` is the shared POOL and
                not the block, and it failed with `chain: block CID mismatch` rather
                than silently measuring a neighbouring block's bytes."
   :code "The shipped `ks/hydrate-run-persist!` itself, :advanced-optimized."
   :mirror "Phase B is a copy of hydrate!'s fold, so it is checked against
            `ks/hydrate!` over the same bytes with `=` on every run. Disagreement
            aborts instead of reporting a split of something that is not the
            hydrate. It agreed on every run."}
  :units "process.cpuUsage() user+system. Node is NOT workerd -- see :workerd-cpu.
          The RATIO between phases is what transfers."
  :rows
  [{:phase :whole-served-hydrate :cpu-ms [1720 2232] :share 1.00
    :block-gets 84 :distinct 84 :duplicate 0 :bytes 11462579}
   {:phase :a-hot-datoms :cpu-ms [491 538] :share 0.23
    :what "84 block gets + dag-cbor decode + prolly-tree scan + novelty merge"}
   {:phase :b-fold-and-decode2 :cpu-ms [1386 1473] :share 0.64
    :what "group-by :e, then decode2 (edn/read-string TWICE per stored value) and
           build the nested {:docs {coll {k v}}} map"}
   {:phase :remainder :cpu-ms 300 :cpu-ms-note "approximate: whole minus (a + b)" :share 0.13
    :what "head read, before/after :docs diff, the counters"}]
  :rows-scanned 4530
  :seed-shape {:collections 17 :docs 888 :streams 2 :revision 1821}}

 ;; ------------------------------------------- 4. what a materialised seed would cost
 :seed-decode-candidates
 {:note "Each is a whole-seed materialisation keyed by the chain CID. They differ
         only in how many values get parsed, which is the whole finding."
  :rows
  [{:encoding :edn :bytes 4930593 :decode-cpu-ms [887 919]
    :verified "result `=` the hydrate's own state" :vs-hydrate -0.60}
   {:encoding :cbor-structure-plus-edn-values :bytes 4933159 :decode-cpu-ms [836 951]
    :verified ":docs `=` the hydrate's :docs" :vs-hydrate -0.57}
   {:encoding :cbor-structure-only-values-never-parsed :bytes 4933159
    :decode-cpu-ms [2.5 6.8] :vs-hydrate -0.997
    :caveat "NOT a usable seed on its own -- LocalStore would hand a handler a
             string where a map belongs. Measured as the FLOOR: what the decode
             costs if value parsing can be made per-collection or lazy."}]
  :reading "A seed materialisation removes phase A outright. Phase B survives ANY
            encoding that has to produce the plain Clojure map a synchronous
            LocalStore holds. What separates -60% from -99.7% is not where the
            bytes come from; it is how many values get parsed."}

